00:12:02,640 --> 00:12:03,640
It's in the past.

261
00:12:03,640 --> 00:12:04,640
Yeah.

262
00:12:04,640 --> 00:12:10,760
And you commonly use this for when somebody is upset about a small thing, something they shouldn't worry about.

263
00:12:10,760 --> 00:12:11,230
Get over it.

264
00:12:11,760 --> 00:12:12,760
Yep.

265
00:12:12,760 --> 00:12:13,330
Now, why don't we go on to our final word?

266
00:12:13,760 --> 00:12:17,770
At the end of the dialogue, they said, I'm going to open a can of whoop ass.

267
00:12:18,200 --> 00:12:18,490
Okay.

268
00:12:18,590 --> 00:12:21,770
So to open a can of whoop ass on somebody.

269
00:12:22,090 --> 00:12:23,470
To open a can of whoop ass.

270
00:12:23,810 --> 00:12:26,470
So again, let's break this down before we explain it.

271
00:12:26,680 --> 00:12:26,940
Okay.

272
00:12:27,100 --> 00:12:28,980
I think we have to take one step back here.

273
00:12:29,620 --> 00:12:33,280
To whoop someone's ass is to beat them up, right?

274
00:12:33,760 --> 00:12:34,760
Right.

275
00:12:34,760 --> 00:12:35,760
To punch them and hurt them.

276
00:12:35,760 --> 00:12:35,960
Right.

277
00:12:36,080 --> 00:12:37,700
So to whoop somebody's ass.

278
00:12:38,640 --> 00:12:41,800
So if I open a can of whoop ass, what does that mean?

279
00:12:41,800 --> 00:12:43,800
It means you're going to beat them up.

280
00:12:43,800 --> 00:12:50,220
So it's just a funny, very informal way of saying like, in this can, I'm going to open it and I'm going to start hitting you or something like that.

281
00:12:50,520 --> 00:12:53,860
Some sort of small man is going to jump out of the can and start hitting you.

282
00:12:54,520 --> 00:12:55,520
Exactly.

283
00:12:55,520 --> 00:12:56,480
But it's never literal.

284
00:12:57,040 --> 00:12:59,140
It's not like you're actually going to beat somebody up.

285
00:12:59,860 --> 00:13:00,860
It's just kind of like a joke.

286
00:13:00,860 --> 00:13:01,860
Yeah.

287
00:13:01,860 --> 00:13:02,860
It's an empty threat.

288
00:13:02,860 --> 00:13:03,860
Yeah.

289
00:13:03,860 --> 00:13:04,860
All right.

290
00:13:04,860 --> 00:13:06,860
So some really interesting words and phrases in this dialogue.

291
00:13:07,120 --> 00:13:13,380
I think we should listen to it for the last time and then we'll come back and talk a little bit more about this great decade.

292
00:13:28,940 --> 00:13:30,400
Hey, Four Eyes.

293
00:13:31,360 --> 00:13:31,880
What's up, man?

294
00:13:32,640 --> 00:13:33,460
How have you been?

295
00:13:33,880 --> 00:13:34,780
Not bad.

296
00:13:35,560 --> 00:13:37,840
Just went to the mall and picked up some junk.

297
00:13:38,300 --> 00:13:39,620
Check out my new Adidas.

298
00:13:40,380 --> 00:13:41,340
Those are dope.

299
00:13:42,360 --> 00:13:46,020
You are going to be getting mad props from the gang, man.

300
00:13:47,000 --> 00:13:49,050
Anyways, have you seen Betty lately?

301
00:13:49,880 --> 00:13:52,640
Dude, don't even go there.

302
00:13:52,640 --> 00:13:56,630
That girl started tripping because I went to the movies with Veronica the other day.

303
00:13:57,240 --> 00:14:01,570
I was like, look, you knew how I was before you got with me.

304
00:14:02,440 --> 00:14:02,710
That's right.

305
00:14:03,440 --> 00:14:06,040
You are such a player, man.

306
00:14:06,040 --> 00:14:07,290
Dude, there's Mad Max.

307
00:14:08,120 --> 00:14:08,890
Let's go say hi.

308
00:14:11,080 --> 00:14:13,040
Max, what's up?

309
00:14:13,040 --> 00:14:13,500
Are you okay?

310
00:14:14,240 --> 00:14:15,660
You look like you just saw a ghost.

311
00:14:16,200 --> 00:14:17,860
I got an F in English class.

312
00:14:18,420 --> 00:14:19,420
My life is over.

313
00:14:19,960 --> 00:14:22,480
Dude, you're over it.

314
00:14:22,480 --> 00:14:25,760
You need to lay off the books for a while and have some fun.

315
00:14:26,600 --> 00:14:27,960
Come on, let's bounce.

316
00:14:28,880 --> 00:14:29,320
Where are we going?

317
00:14:30,040 --> 00:14:30,500
Oh, crap.

318
00:14:30,880 --> 00:14:34,600
My dad is going to go postal when he finds out about this.

319
00:14:34,600 --> 00:14:40,000
I'm going to open a can of whoop-ass on you if you don't come with me right now.

320
00:14:40,180 --> 00:14:41,000
Okay, okay.

321
00:14:41,000 --> 00:14:42,000
Geez.

322
00:14:51,490 --> 00:14:56,130
Well, Marco, in the dialogue, I heard one of the guys say, what's up?

323
00:14:56,310 --> 00:14:58,470
But he said it in a really strange way, didn't he?

324
00:14:59,010 --> 00:14:59,450
Yeah, exactly.

325
00:14:59,830 --> 00:15:00,930
He said, what's up?

326
00:15:01,450 --> 00:15:01,910
Okay.

327
00:15:02,150 --> 00:15:03,910
So what's up with that?

328
00:15:04,390 --> 00:15:10,670
So in the 90s, it was popular instead of saying what's up, you would just kind of double the S and just say what's up.

329
00:15:11,360 --> 00:15:16,120
And then it became very famous with a TV commercial for a certain beer.

330
00:15:16,640 --> 00:15:19,880
And actually, I remember this commercial during the Super Bowl.

331
00:15:20,200 --> 00:15:26,680
They had this big commercial where everyone was saying what's up in this really strange way, right?

332
00:15:26,680 --> 00:15:28,660
It's still used today informally between friends.

333
00:15:28,960 --> 00:15:30,320
You'll go ahead, hey, what's up?

334
00:15:30,960 --> 00:15:31,260
What's up?

335
00:15:31,960 --> 00:15:32,940
Yeah, you'll use it.

336
00:15:33,180 --> 00:15:36,580
Yeah, so that commercial made it actually quite cool and famous to talk like that.

337
00:15:37,040 --> 00:15:42,260
Actually in SpanishPod, they have a show called Que Pasa, which is like the translation of what's up.

338
00:15:42,880 --> 00:15:43,880
Oh, right.

339
00:15:43,880 --> 00:15:47,640
They start each show with like saying, Que Pasa, but it's exactly the same.

340
00:15:47,640 --> 00:15:49,780
So maybe it will become popular in Spanish.

341
00:15:50,480 --> 00:15:51,420
Maybe but I guess only...

342
00:15:52,600 --> 00:15:52,970
Time will tell.

343
00:15:53,600 --> 00:15:54,600
Yeah, that's right.

344
00:15:54,600 --> 00:15:55,600
All right.

345
00:15:55,600 --> 00:15:55,890
So really cool stuff.

346
00:15:56,600 --> 00:15:58,990
And as we heard typical music from the 90s.

347
00:15:59,240 --> 00:16:04,450
And another interesting thing was the nicknames, Four Eyes, Mad Max, very popular among young kids.

348
00:16:05,160 --> 00:16:12,400
So remember, if you have any questions about this language or anything else about this lesson, come to our website at EnglishPod.com.

349
00:16:12,890 --> 00:16:13,130
Right.

350
